doesn't happen right away.

00:00:07.863 --> 00:00:09.306
Number one mistake so many people make

00:00:09.306 --> 00:00:11.642
is they care about what other people have.

00:00:11.642 --> 00:00:13.453
I don't give a (beep) what anybody has.

00:00:13.453 --> 00:00:14.800
If I have more, if I have less.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.823
It doesn't matter, I worry about myself.

00:00:16.823 --> 00:00:19.080
- He's an American entrepreneur, investor

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.050
and four times New York
Times best selling author.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.565
He's best known as a digital marketer

00:00:23.565 --> 00:00:25.411
and social media pioneer.

00:00:25.411 --> 00:00:27.939
He's an angel investor or advisor to Uber

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:29.982
Birchbox, Twitter, Facebook

00:00:29.982 --> 00:00:31.583
Snapchat, and Tumblr.

00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:32.436
He's Gary Vaynerchuk

00:00:32.436 --> 00:00:34.468
and here's my take on his top 10 rules

00:00:34.468 --> 00:00:36.360
for success volume two.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.723
Rule number three is my personal favorite

00:00:37.723 --> 00:00:40.093
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:40.093 --> 00:00:41.520
And as always guys, if you hear something

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.093
that really resonates with you

00:00:43.093 --> 00:00:44.892
please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:00:44.892 --> 00:00:46.067
Put quotes around it as well

00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:47.811
so other people can be inspired.

00:00:47.811 --> 00:00:49.113
Also when you write it down

00:00:49.113 --> 00:00:50.651
you're much more likely to lock it in

00:00:50.651 --> 00:00:52.984
for yourself as well, enjoy.

00:00:56.153 --> 00:00:59.570
(upbeat inspiring music)

00:01:06.088 --> 00:01:08.395
- What if I told you this
was the last Monday morning

00:01:08.395 --> 00:01:09.250
of your life?

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:10.996
What if I told you you'd die this week?

00:01:10.996 --> 00:01:13.029
Would you complain about your crap job

00:01:13.029 --> 00:01:15.029
or that test you don't want to take?

00:01:15.029 --> 00:01:15.932
I doubt it.

00:01:15.932 --> 00:01:18.407
You'd go much higher level thinking.

00:01:18.407 --> 00:01:19.931
Well that's really what it takes.

00:01:19.931 --> 00:01:23.934
It takes understanding that if
you're not pumped right now.

00:01:23.934 --> 00:01:25.981
If you're begrudging
what you're about to do.

00:01:25.981 --> 00:01:27.891
If you're not looking forward to it.

00:01:27.891 --> 00:01:30.111
Look, I respect practicality.

00:01:30.111 --> 00:01:31.243
You got to go through school

00:01:31.243 --> 00:01:32.265
because your parents want to.

00:01:32.265 --> 00:01:33.480
You got to pay your rent.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.655
You got student loans.

00:01:34.655 --> 00:01:35.488
I get it.

00:01:35.488 --> 00:01:37.867
But please recognize the
world we're living in.

00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:38.719
We're living in a world where

00:01:38.719 --> 00:01:40.564
there is so much more opportunity.

00:01:40.564 --> 00:01:43.407
This internet thing created
way more opportunity

00:01:43.407 --> 00:01:45.249
for all of us, way more.

00:01:45.249 --> 00:01:47.083
And look, you might not even be alive.

00:01:47.083 --> 00:01:49.086
Like, your mom and dad could have had sex

00:01:49.086 --> 00:01:50.331
like, three minutes later

00:01:50.331 --> 00:01:51.884
and you wouldn't even exist.

00:01:51.884 --> 00:01:53.080
And you're complaining.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.614
You could have ended
up being a bus, a tree.

00:01:55.614 --> 00:01:57.566
I just don't get the mentality

00:01:57.566 --> 00:02:00.666
of being head down sad
on a Monday morning.

00:02:00.666 --> 00:02:02.795
I'm going to make Monday
morning my (beep).

00:02:02.795 --> 00:02:04.606
I'm going to make you
Saturday, Monday morning.

00:02:04.606 --> 00:02:06.266
That's what I want to do every morning

00:02:06.266 --> 00:02:07.922
and that's what I want from you.

00:02:07.922 --> 00:02:09.367
Please, take a step back

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:12.509
and think about how
awesome it actually is.

00:02:12.509 --> 00:02:15.137
And then, recognize that
you can attack the world

00:02:15.137 --> 00:02:16.678
in a totally different way

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:18.718
because you are lucky enough to be born

00:02:18.718 --> 00:02:20.051
during this era.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:22.233
So many people come up to me and say

00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:23.066
"Gary, it's so hard."

00:02:23.066 --> 00:02:24.366
I look at their Instagram

00:02:24.366 --> 00:02:27.510
and they're not posting any
of their content with hashtags

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.636
which is a by accident
way to pick up exposure

00:02:30.636 --> 00:02:32.482
in a world where they have no money.

00:02:32.482 --> 00:02:33.315
When you have no money.

00:02:33.315 --> 00:02:34.270
When I took over my dad's business

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:36.838
it did $3 million a
year, 10% gross profit.

00:02:36.838 --> 00:02:38.522
$300,000 before expenses.

00:02:38.522 --> 00:02:41.437
My first year marketing
budget was $14,000.

00:02:41.437 --> 00:02:42.648
When you have no money.

00:02:42.648 --> 00:02:43.752
And I built that business from a three

00:02:43.752 --> 00:02:46.356
to a $60 million business in five years.

00:02:46.356 --> 00:02:48.452
I had to make every penny perfect.

00:02:48.452 --> 00:02:50.480
So I was right about
email marketing in '96

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.252
and I was right, because of
that I had 91% open rates.

00:02:54.252 --> 00:02:56.579
When Google Adwords came
out, the day it came out

00:02:56.579 --> 00:02:59.070
I paid five cents a click for words

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.371
before anybody bid me up

00:03:00.371 --> 00:03:02.681
and I was super right and that worked.

00:03:02.681 --> 00:03:04.592
- How much is that luck versus preparation

00:03:04.592 --> 00:03:06.341
and do you ever get anything wrong?

00:03:06.341 --> 00:03:07.521
What do you do when you make a mistake?

00:03:07.521 --> 00:03:08.827
- I get everything wrong.

00:03:08.827 --> 00:03:10.525
It's just that I can't recall it

00:03:10.525 --> 00:03:13.254
because once it's wrong I'm
moving onto the next thing.

00:03:13.254 --> 00:03:16.408
Like, dwelling on what you (beep) up on

00:03:16.408 --> 00:03:19.770
is the quickest way for
the next thing not to work.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:20.603
Right?

00:03:20.603 --> 00:03:21.436
- Yup.

00:03:21.436 --> 00:03:22.269
(applause and cheering)

00:03:22.269 --> 00:03:23.285
- So I think I do everything.

00:03:23.285 --> 00:03:24.671
I mean, you know this.

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:25.784
This is a fun thing to say.

00:03:25.784 --> 00:03:27.157
Some people in the back know this.

00:03:27.157 --> 00:03:30.199
I was a break-out Youtube
star in the first year.

00:03:30.199 --> 00:03:33.434
2006, I decided that the right strategy

00:03:33.434 --> 00:03:35.537
was to leave Youtube completely

00:03:35.537 --> 00:03:37.093
and go to Viddler

00:03:37.093 --> 00:03:39.792
because Viddler offered
me equity in their company

00:03:39.792 --> 00:03:42.355
and I've left an enormous
amount of attention.

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:44.420
I deviated from my intention thesis

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:45.860
to do short-term economics

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:47.771
in equity in a company.

00:03:47.771 --> 00:03:49.393
And I lost.

00:03:49.393 --> 00:03:50.226
I lost.

00:03:50.226 --> 00:03:51.473
When D-Rock finally came
into my life two years ago

00:03:51.473 --> 00:03:52.899
and we started to try
to build up my Youtube

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:53.732
for the first time

00:03:53.732 --> 00:03:55.670
I was sitting on 40,000 followers

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:57.140
in a world where I could have had millions

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:58.473
if I had just stayed the course.

00:03:58.473 --> 00:04:00.444
So I make mistakes all the time.

00:04:00.444 --> 00:04:02.551
I'm re-working VaynerMedia everyday

00:04:02.551 --> 00:04:04.866
because it's based on a
mistake I made the prior year.

00:04:04.866 --> 00:04:07.180
I just don't give a
(beep) about my mistakes.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.826
Everybody else cares about your mistakes.

00:04:08.826 --> 00:04:10.066
If you're worried about your own mistakes

00:04:10.066 --> 00:04:11.448
you've already lost.

00:04:11.448 --> 00:04:14.337
- Sometimes a lot of people
may try a new initiative

00:04:14.337 --> 00:04:16.140
and then they don't get
an immediate reaction

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:17.661
and they think it's a failure.

00:04:17.661 --> 00:04:18.494
- [Gary] That's right.

00:04:18.494 --> 00:04:20.002
- I watch your Youtube videos

00:04:20.002 --> 00:04:21.189
with the Wine Library.

00:04:21.189 --> 00:04:22.988
You know, you had so
much content out there.

00:04:22.988 --> 00:04:24.208
How did you stick in there?

00:04:24.208 --> 00:04:26.914
How did you keep yourself motivated?

00:04:26.914 --> 00:04:28.277
- Because I believe in my strategies

00:04:28.277 --> 00:04:29.947
and I'm patient as (beep).

00:04:29.947 --> 00:04:31.697
Like, all great stuff

00:04:32.574 --> 00:04:33.732
especially when you're innovating

00:04:33.732 --> 00:04:35.598
doesn't happen right away.

00:04:35.598 --> 00:04:37.520
This notion of like, "Oh Gary, I tried it.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.508
"I spent 500 bucks on Facebook
ads, it doesn't work."

00:04:40.508 --> 00:04:41.595
Well what if your video sucked?

00:04:41.595 --> 00:04:43.406
