And so we just need a call to action

00:09:29.287 --> 00:09:31.117
for everybody to recognize

00:09:31.117 --> 00:09:33.995
we have to stop judging everybody else.

00:09:33.995 --> 00:09:35.403
I'm not judging you.

00:09:35.403 --> 00:09:38.180
I'm creating content for
you to use as a barometer

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:39.707
to try to figure out what you like

00:09:39.707 --> 00:09:40.540
and what you don't like

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:41.963
and I'm trying to provide value

00:09:41.963 --> 00:09:43.911
through my words, my actions

00:09:43.911 --> 00:09:46.286
and my content to bring to people

00:09:46.286 --> 00:09:47.965
to amplify their worlds.

00:09:47.965 --> 00:09:49.054
And that's really it.

00:09:49.054 --> 00:09:50.275
That's the clarification.

00:09:50.275 --> 00:09:53.527
Like, I don't want people to be like me.

00:09:53.527 --> 00:09:55.458
I don't want people to do me.

00:09:55.458 --> 00:09:57.195
I want people to do themselves.

00:09:57.195 --> 00:09:59.985
And they just have to start
with understanding themselves

00:09:59.985 --> 00:10:02.175
and not trying to be
like me in this moment

00:10:02.175 --> 00:10:03.321
or that person the next moment.

00:10:03.321 --> 00:10:04.743
Or that person the next moment.

00:10:04.743 --> 00:10:07.282
The people that most win
are in tune with themselves

00:10:07.282 --> 00:10:09.668
and figure themselves out.

00:10:09.668 --> 00:10:12.331
The problem is, if you
have the greatest hammer

00:10:12.331 --> 00:10:13.981
and the greatest screwdriver

00:10:13.981 --> 00:10:16.183
and the greatest wrench, the greatest

00:10:16.183 --> 00:10:17.532
in front of you.

00:10:17.532 --> 00:10:19.595
If you don't use them properly

00:10:19.595 --> 00:10:20.428
you will lose.

00:10:20.428 --> 00:10:21.261
- [Host] Right.

00:10:21.261 --> 00:10:22.692
- If there's a nail that I
have to put in that ground

00:10:22.692 --> 00:10:24.432
but I take the wrench and I hit it

00:10:24.432 --> 00:10:26.384
when the hammer was right there.

00:10:26.384 --> 00:10:28.314
It still comes down to the practitioner

00:10:28.314 --> 00:10:30.687
and you have to be good at your craft.

00:10:30.687 --> 00:10:33.659
And you have to love your,
if you don't love sales

00:10:33.659 --> 00:10:36.123
if you don't love it, you have no chance.

00:10:36.123 --> 00:10:37.621
Because it's so hard, it's so painful.

00:10:37.621 --> 00:10:39.193
There's so much rejection.

00:10:39.193 --> 00:10:40.891
- [Host] Right.

00:10:40.891 --> 00:10:42.451
- So those are the things I think about.

00:10:42.451 --> 00:10:44.961
- So, technology has enabled
as you just said there

00:10:44.961 --> 00:10:48.022
that the person is equally important.

00:10:48.022 --> 00:10:48.950
- [Gary] More important.

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:49.783
- Right, and going back to--

00:10:49.783 --> 00:10:50.616
- More important.

00:10:50.616 --> 00:10:52.878
Like I apologize for jumping in

00:10:52.878 --> 00:10:54.594
but let me make it perfectly straight.

00:10:54.594 --> 00:10:57.712
If I go play Roger Federer
right now in tennis

00:10:57.712 --> 00:10:59.835
and I have the greatest
tennis racket ever made.

00:10:59.835 --> 00:11:03.002
Like taken for the Mar's new resources

00:11:04.101 --> 00:11:07.947
and he took a John
McEnroe 1974 tennis racket

00:11:07.947 --> 00:11:09.221
he would whip my ass.

00:11:09.221 --> 00:11:10.209
(laughs)

00:11:10.209 --> 00:11:12.182
Like, I am crushing sales people

00:11:12.182 --> 00:11:15.053
that have every sales force,
every one of your competitors

00:11:15.053 --> 00:11:18.835
37 features, upgraded, went
to every PRO conference

00:11:18.835 --> 00:11:20.236
watching this.

00:11:20.236 --> 00:11:22.628
I will beat their ass in
sales every day of the week.

00:11:22.628 --> 00:11:23.757
Pure talent.

00:11:23.757 --> 00:11:24.907
- [Audience Member]
Because you regularly say

00:11:24.907 --> 00:11:26.794
that failure propels you.

00:11:26.794 --> 00:11:29.324
Are there any instances in your past truly

00:11:29.324 --> 00:11:31.817
where you couldn't get over it and why?

00:11:31.817 --> 00:11:32.650
- Nope.

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.744
(audience laughs)

00:11:35.744 --> 00:11:36.577
- [Audience Member] And that's why--

00:11:36.577 --> 00:11:38.660
- By the way, by the way!

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:43.045
That's the same answer for
everybody in this room.

00:11:43.045 --> 00:11:44.762
That's what humans are good at.

00:11:44.762 --> 00:11:47.474
We all fail, we've all failed.

00:11:47.474 --> 00:11:48.807
Like, there are.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.729
My parents haven't been killed, my child.

00:11:52.729 --> 00:11:55.589
I mean, there's people that
have their children die.

00:11:55.589 --> 00:11:57.149
Like could you imagine?

00:11:57.149 --> 00:11:58.833
Like the (beep) I deal with.

00:11:58.833 --> 00:12:01.431
Listen, unfortunately or fortunately

00:12:01.431 --> 00:12:03.312
depends on how one wants to look at it

00:12:03.312 --> 00:12:04.531
I'm a pretty optimistic guy.

00:12:04.531 --> 00:12:06.296
I lost three of my four grandparents

00:12:06.296 --> 00:12:08.111
before I was really, before I knew them.

00:12:08.111 --> 00:12:10.080
Two before I was born
and one very early on.

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:13.924
So, you know it's easy for me to be happy.

00:12:13.924 --> 00:12:16.927
I have my (beep) in the right order.

00:12:16.927 --> 00:12:19.233
The health and well being of my family.

00:12:19.233 --> 00:12:22.632
And there's nothing even
remotely close to that.

00:12:22.632 --> 00:12:24.699
Not even remotely.

00:12:24.699 --> 00:12:27.086
People have gotten through much worse.

00:12:27.086 --> 00:12:29.098
This is why I'm trying to
recall our grandparents

00:12:29.098 --> 00:12:30.449
and our great-grandparents.

00:12:30.449 --> 00:12:32.543
People were persecuted and killed

00:12:32.543 --> 00:12:35.705
and like I just, we do
not have real headaches.

00:12:35.705 --> 00:12:38.806
You know, I don't know why I have this

00:12:38.806 --> 00:12:41.608
highly emotional and then
completely un-emotional

00:12:41.608 --> 00:12:42.461
part of me

00:12:42.461 --> 00:12:44.652
but I mix them together
to create the balance

00:12:44.652 --> 00:12:45.516
and that's what works for me.

00:12:45.516 --> 00:12:47.122
So of course there's nothing

00:12:47.122 --> 00:12:49.696
that I couldn't get up from.

00:12:49.696 --> 00:12:52.373
Like every other human
ever besides somebody

00:12:52.373 --> 00:12:53.915
who now lives in some weird cave

00:12:53.915 --> 00:12:55.079
that we don't know about.

00:12:55.079 --> 00:12:56.291
(audience laughs)

00:12:56.291 --> 00:12:57.124
You know what I mean?

00:12:57.124 --> 00:12:57.957
- [Woman] I do.

00:12:57.957 --> 00:12:59.672
- We are strong as (beep).

00:12:59.672 --> 00:13:01.641
We're really (beep) strong.

00:13:01.641 --> 00:13:03.678
We're just being sold that we're not.

00:13:03.678 --> 00:13:05.722
Because there's a lot of money.

00:13:05.722 --> 00:13:09.414
A lot of money in telling us
that we're not pretty enough

00:13:09.414 --> 00:13:13.193
thin enough, smart enough, good enough.

00:13:13.193 --> 00:13:14.064
(beep) that.

00:13:14.064 --> 00:13:15.765
I want to tell you you're the (beep) best.

00:13:15.765 --> 00:13:16.996
Go do (beep).

00:13:16.996 --> 00:13:19.489
- [Woman] I've been feeling a little bit

00:13:19.489 --> 00:13:21.206
unmotivated and I've been feeling

00:13:21.206 --> 00:13:22.971
like I should be doing something

00:13:22.971 --> 00:13:25.411
or I should be, just like I feel like

00:13:25.411 --> 00:13:27.560
I should be pulled in different.

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:29.102
I'm feeling, uninspired basically.

00:13:29.102 --> 00:13:29.935
That's my question.

00:13:29.935 --> 00:13:33.209
How do you get, when you lose inspiration

00:13:33.209 --> 00:13:35.671
or you lose motivation,
how do you get it back?

00:13:35.671 --> 00:13:39.838
- I get it back by remembering
that you might die tomorrow.

00:13:41.049 --> 00:13:42.569
- [Woman] That's true.

00:13:42.569 --> 00:13:46.003
- So, I'm inspired by practicality

00:13:46.003 --> 00:13:47.829
and reality and the truth

00:13:47.829 --> 00:13:50.307
which is it is ridiculously impossible

00:13:50.307 --> 00:13:51.715
to become a human being.

00:13:51.715 --> 00:13:52.741
I am one.

00:13:52.741 --> 00:13:55.622
I have other good things
going for me as a human being.

00:13:55.622 --> 00:13:56.836
And I'm just grateful.

00:13:56.836 --> 00:14:00.591
You're dwelling and looking
at what you don't have

00:14:00.591 --> 00:14:02.164
versus looking at what you have.

00:14:02.164 --> 00:14:05.533
If you actually looked at what you had

00:14:05.533 --> 00:14:08.584
your health, you know living in America.

00:14:08.584 --> 00:14:10.760
You know, who the heck knows
what you be on your list

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:13.994
if you actually spent all
your time looking at that

00:14:13.994 --> 00:14:16.610
versus, "I don't have a million dollars.

00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:18.250
"I'm not famous.

00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:20.184
"I don't have this."

00:14:20.184 --> 00:14:22.117
If you spent all your
time on what you have

00:14:22.117 --> 00:14:23.445
versus what you don't have

00:14:23.445 --> 00:14:25.828
you would be the happiest girl on Earth.

00:14:25.828 --> 00:14:27.682
- [Man] Should I do this on the side

00:14:27.682 --> 00:14:29.853
or take a gap year and try to launch it?

00:14:29.853 --> 00:14:30.686
- Both work.

00:14:30.686 --> 00:14:31.519
- [Man] Both? Okay.

00:14:31.519 --> 00:14:32.352
- Both work.

00:14:32.352 --> 00:14:33.185
- [Man] Which one is best?

00:14:33.185 --> 00:14:35.643
- I don't know you well enough, you know.

00:14:35.643 --> 00:14:36.903
You know what I mean?

00:14:36.903 --> 00:14:37.736
They both work.

00:14:37.736 --> 00:14:38.569
- [Man] Yes.

00:14:38.569 --> 00:14:39.402
- Which one do you want to do?

00:14:39.402 --> 00:14:41.382
- [Man] I would like to
try to take a gap year.

00:14:41.382 --> 00:14:42.215
- So take a gap year.

00:14:42.215 --> 00:14:43.048
- [Man] But I'm scared.

00:14:43.048 --> 00:14:44.489
- Well that's why you're asking.

00:14:44.489 --> 00:14:47.205
- [Man] Yeah, which one will you do?

00:14:47.205 --> 00:14:49.569
- I wouldn't take either.

00:14:49.569 --> 00:14:50.557
- [Man] What will you do then?

00:14:50.557 --> 00:14:52.303
- I can't answer that for you.

00:14:52.303 --> 00:14:53.445
Fear is holding you back.

00:14:53.445 --> 00:14:55.134
You know what you want to do.

00:14:55.134 --> 00:14:56.082
The only reason you're not doing it

00:14:56.082 --> 00:14:56.919
is because you're scared of doing

00:14:56.919 --> 00:14:58.566
what you actually want to do.

00:14:58.566 --> 00:14:59.591
Right?

00:14:59.591 --> 00:15:03.292
Are you scared to do it because
your parents won't like it?

00:15:03.292 --> 00:15:05.152
- [Man] No, my parents
actually support me.

00:15:05.152 --> 00:15:07.173
- Okay, so what are
you scared of? Failing?

00:15:07.173 --> 00:15:09.891
- [Man] Not failing but um,

00:15:09.891 --> 00:15:11.600
still being in the same place

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:14.461
when my friends are at the next level.

00:15:14.461 --> 00:15:15.809
- Huge mistake.

00:15:15.809 --> 00:15:17.277
You don't want the same
thing your friends want.

00:15:17.277 --> 00:15:18.110
- [Man] No.

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:19.974
- So why the (beep) do
you care what they have?

00:15:19.974 --> 00:15:21.419
Number one mistake so many people make

00:15:21.419 --> 00:15:23.692
is they care about what other people have.

00:15:23.692 --> 00:15:25.421
I don't give a (beep) what anybody has.

00:15:25.421 --> 00:15:26.838
If I have more, if I have less.

00:15:26.838 --> 00:15:28.421
It doesn't matter, I worry about myself.

00:15:28.421 --> 00:15:29.982
The fact that you're worrying about

00:15:29.982 --> 00:15:31.202
other people's accomplishments

00:15:31.202 --> 00:15:32.705
is already a losing formula.

00:15:32.705 --> 00:15:33.791
You need to get rid of that first

00:15:33.791 --> 00:15:35.269
then you can focus on yourself.

00:15:35.269 --> 00:15:36.102
- [Man] Thank you.

00:15:36.102 --> 00:15:36.935
- You understand?

00:15:36.935 --> 00:15:40.992
You know to be great, I
think you have to fight.

00:15:40.992 --> 00:15:43.358
And I very much thing that my success

00:15:43.358 --> 00:15:46.362
is a product of some level of skill.

00:15:46.362 --> 00:15:49.348
But I do think I win
because I outwork people.

00:15:49.348 --> 00:15:50.404
I really do believe that.

00:15:50.404 --> 00:15:53.225
I do believe that 100%.

00:15:53.225 --> 00:15:57.133
And I'm not sure that if my
dad didn't set that example

00:15:57.133 --> 00:15:59.138
that I'd even have the ability to think

00:15:59.138 --> 00:16:01.221
one could work that hard.

00:16:03.305 --> 00:16:06.476
The fact that I've been
working 19 hours a day

00:16:06.476 --> 00:16:09.657
every day for the last
20 years is easy for me.

00:16:09.657 --> 00:16:11.268
It's the only gear I do right?

00:16:11.268 --> 00:16:14.286
I was poor, I sucked (beep) at school.

00:16:14.286 --> 00:16:15.847
It was the only gear I had.

00:16:15.847 --> 00:16:17.585
You know I think you need to recognize

00:16:17.585 --> 00:16:19.835
that your biggest advantage

00:16:20.865 --> 00:16:23.350
is that you're hungrier
than your competitor

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:27.841
and that if you're not
applying your one advantage

00:16:27.841 --> 00:16:29.428
which is your work ethic

00:16:29.428 --> 00:16:32.223
and the hours that you have
to put into your business

00:16:32.223 --> 00:16:33.663
well then you're going to come up short.

00:16:33.663 --> 00:16:36.868
I sit here with enormous assumptions

00:16:36.868 --> 00:16:38.074
around all of you

00:16:38.074 --> 00:16:40.947
that you're just too soft to beat me.

00:16:40.947 --> 00:16:43.173
Right, but I think that
you've had it better.

00:16:43.173 --> 00:16:45.934
And that that alone doesn't
allow you to beat me.

00:16:45.934 --> 00:16:48.247
Somebody will come with the
counter culture point of view

00:16:48.247 --> 00:16:49.808
and be like, "Gary that's cool

00:16:49.808 --> 00:16:52.211
"But I don't have to work that hard

00:16:52.211 --> 00:16:53.901
"Because I'm working smarter."

00:16:53.901 --> 00:16:54.928
Yeah me too (beep).

00:16:54.928 --> 00:16:57.595
I work hard and smart, now what?

00:16:58.570 --> 00:17:00.639
Look, there's a 12 hour, 10 hour

00:17:00.639 --> 00:17:03.197
eight hour, 15 hour work day.

00:17:03.197 --> 00:17:04.711
You can finish a lot of things

00:17:04.711 --> 00:17:07.237
in those 18, 12, nine hours.

00:17:07.237 --> 00:17:08.914
Or you can finish medium amounts of things

00:17:08.914 --> 00:17:10.418
or lightweight things.

00:17:10.418 --> 00:17:13.135
People focus on too many small details.

00:17:13.135 --> 00:17:14.371
Way too many people in this room

00:17:14.371 --> 00:17:16.111
are going to spend the next
30, 40 years of their lives

00:17:16.111 --> 00:17:17.477
trying to check the boxes

00:17:17.477 --> 00:17:19.087
on the things that they're not as good at

00:17:19.087 --> 00:17:20.205
and that, you're going to waste

00:17:20.205 --> 00:17:22.332
a (beep) load of time and lose.

00:17:22.332 --> 00:17:24.480
I highly recommend, for all you hustlers

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:25.738
because there's a lot of you.

00:17:25.738 --> 00:17:26.804
There's a lot of you

00:17:26.804 --> 00:17:29.230
that are always talking
about, "Gary I do work hard."

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:31.404
And you do, you work for 16 hours.

00:17:31.404 --> 00:17:34.190
Some people just don't
have the attention span

00:17:34.190 --> 00:17:36.440
or the capacity to remember

00:17:37.494 --> 00:17:39.579
or like, there's a lot
of things I can't learn.

00:17:39.579 --> 00:17:41.150
I was a very poor student

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:43.505
because the subject matter bored me.

00:17:43.505 --> 00:17:46.344
And if I was forced to become great

00:17:46.344 --> 00:17:49.807
at understanding the great
artists of the 20th century

00:17:49.807 --> 00:17:51.038
I'm in big trouble.

00:17:51.038 --> 00:17:54.560
And so I would tell people
to bet on their strengths.

00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:56.285
You need to bet on your strengths

00:17:56.285 --> 00:17:58.399
and don't give a (beep)
about what you suck at.

00:17:58.399 --> 00:18:00.028
And to put themselves in a position

00:18:00.028 --> 00:18:02.186
to win with their strengths

00:18:02.186 --> 00:18:06.353
because that is absolutely the
straightest line to success.

00:18:08.764 --> 00:18:10.779
Greatness comes from adversity

00:18:10.779 --> 00:18:13.779
and looking the challenge in the eye

00:18:14.949 --> 00:18:16.826
and having the intestinal fortitude

00:18:16.826 --> 00:18:20.076
to kind of, to step up and go after it.

00:18:21.518 --> 00:18:24.435
(soft piano music)

00:18:31.349 --> 00:18:32.555
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:18:32.555 --> 00:18:34.674
I'd love to know what did
you think of this video?

00:18:34.674 --> 00:18:36.312
How do you like our volume two series?

00:18:36.312 --> 00:18:37.858
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:18:37.858 --> 00:18:39.344
I'd also love to know what did you learn

00:18:39.344 --> 00:18:40.198
from Gary today?

00:18:40.198 --> 00:18:42.499
What did he say that had
the biggest impact on you?

00:18:42.499 --> 00:18:44.275
What lesson is your favorite

00:18:44.275 --> 00:18:46.301
and you're going to apply
immediately to your life

00:18:46.301 --> 00:18:47.213
or business somehow?

00:18:47.213 --> 00:18:48.841
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:18:48.841 --> 00:18:50.105
I'm super curious to find out.

00:18:50.105 --> 00:18:51.291
Finally, I want to give a quick shout-out

00:18:51.291 --> 00:18:52.277
to Richard Galvez.

00:18:52.277 --> 00:18:53.879
Richard, thank you so
much for picking up a copy

00:18:53.879 --> 00:18:55.568
of my book, Your One Word.

00:18:55.568 --> 00:18:57.292
It really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:18:57.292 --> 00:18:58.743
And I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:18:58.743 --> 00:19:00.030
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:01.446
I believe in you.

00:19:01.446 --> 00:19:03.484
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:19:03.484 --> 00:19:05.252
and whatever your one word is.

00:19:05.252 --> 00:19:07.669
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:19:13.985 --> 00:19:16.739
- [COLSAUCE] Amy asks, "A
professor once said to me

00:19:16.739 --> 00:19:19.144
"It's better to be a big
fish in a small pond.

00:19:19.144 --> 00:19:20.951
"Do you agree?"

00:19:20.951 --> 00:19:25.291
- Amy, this. You know I'm
going to be very upfront

00:19:25.291 --> 00:19:26.270
on this question.

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:28.184
This is a good way to bang out the show.

00:19:28.184 --> 00:19:30.791
That sounds like a loser professor to me.

00:19:30.791 --> 00:19:32.844
It's small time thinking.

00:19:32.844 --> 00:19:34.315
I don't subscribe to it.

00:19:34.315 --> 00:19:36.934
There's a lot of practicality in it right?

00:19:36.934 --> 00:19:38.527
It's a lot easier to be the most

00:19:38.527 --> 00:19:40.884
successful business man
in St. Louis, Missouri

00:19:40.884 --> 00:19:42.792
than it is to be in the world right?

00:19:42.792 --> 00:19:44.602
So I understand the thesis

00:19:44.602 --> 00:19:48.021
but honestly, that to
me is very conservative

00:19:48.021 --> 00:19:49.628
not in my DNA thinking.

00:19:49.628 --> 00:19:51.992
That could be great
advice for a lot of people

00:19:51.992 --> 00:19:54.902
who are delirious and think
they're better than they are.

00:19:54.902 --> 00:19:57.850
Though I love to drive through
self esteem and optimism

00:19:57.850 --> 00:19:59.938
and I think early in your life

00:19:59.938 --> 00:20:01.979
you should absolutely
shoot for the highest stars

00:20:01.979 --> 00:20:02.974
that you can.

00:20:02.974 --> 00:20:04.556
I think as life moves on

00:20:04.556 --> 00:20:05.964
and time moves on

00:20:05.964 --> 00:20:08.446
you need to become more practical.

00:20:08.446 --> 00:20:10.357
You've got bills and kids

00:20:10.357 --> 00:20:11.685
and all those kind of things.

00:20:11.685 --> 00:20:14.765
But to start off one's
career in the first 20 years

00:20:14.765 --> 00:20:15.862
call it till you're 40

00:20:15.862 --> 00:20:16.952
to be in that thinking.

00:20:16.952 --> 00:20:18.257
I think that limits.

00:20:18.257 --> 00:20:19.690
There have been so many people

00:20:19.690 --> 00:20:23.379
who have hedged and that
settled in their lives

00:20:23.379 --> 00:20:25.118
and honestly, I'm not
the kind of character

00:20:25.118 --> 00:20:27.707
that's going to play
in that kind of sphere.

00:20:27.707 --> 00:20:28.859
I'm going in a different direction.

00:20:28.859 --> 00:20:30.067
It's what comes natural to me.

00:20:30.067 --> 00:20:32.023
But it's also because I
think I can be the biggest

00:20:32.023 --> 00:20:34.047
and the baddest in any game I play.

00:20:34.047 --> 00:20:35.590
It's just how I'm wired.

00:20:35.590 --> 00:20:37.157
I think this comes down to

00:20:37.157 --> 00:20:39.276
it's probably very good advice to some

00:20:39.276 --> 00:20:41.310
and it's probably
atrocious advice to others.

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:42.417
And this is why I continue to say

00:20:42.417 --> 00:20:45.670
if I can inject anything into anybody

00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:46.911
it would be self awareness

00:20:46.911 --> 00:20:49.452
because it would help
you answer this question.

00:20:49.452 --> 00:20:50.972
Because for a lot of people, it's probably

00:20:50.972 --> 00:20:52.497
really solid advice.

00:20:52.497 --> 00:20:55.043
For me, and for many of you watching this

00:20:55.043 --> 00:20:56.597
it's crap advice.

00:20:56.597 --> 00:20:58.320
- [AshleyFidget] Allen
asks, "As we've seen

00:20:58.320 --> 00:20:59.616
"You hate wasting time.

00:20:59.616 --> 00:21:00.967
"But how do you adjust on days

00:21:00.967 --> 00:21:03.050
"Nothing is going right?"

00:21:03.961 --> 00:21:04.967
- Well there's no such thing.

00:21:04.967 --> 00:21:06.834
Luckily, I mean there's.

00:21:06.834 --> 00:21:07.667
(knocks on table)

00:21:07.667 --> 00:21:09.842
I don't remember a day
when nothing went right.

00:21:09.842 --> 00:21:10.976
I mean, first and foremost.

00:21:10.976 --> 00:21:12.295
The way I really deal with it

00:21:12.295 --> 00:21:13.296
if we want to get zen about this

00:21:13.296 --> 00:21:14.291
and a lot of you know this.

00:21:14.291 --> 00:21:16.530
Is, as long as everybody's healthy

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:18.111
everything went right.

00:21:18.111 --> 00:21:19.078
I always quantify.

00:21:19.078 --> 00:21:20.857
When business doesn't go well

00:21:20.857 --> 00:21:22.949
I default quickly to

00:21:22.949 --> 00:21:25.513
"Oh business is bull (beep)
it's all about life."

00:21:25.513 --> 00:21:27.607
You know, like how cute my kids are.

00:21:27.607 --> 00:21:29.257
Thank God my parents are alive.

00:21:29.257 --> 00:21:32.340
I quickly switch from, I don't dwell.

00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:35.358
You know, I'm not good at dwelling.

00:21:35.358 --> 00:21:38.264
I love speed and I
don't like wasting time.

00:21:38.264 --> 00:21:40.387
Dwelling, complaining

00:21:40.387 --> 00:21:41.720
wishing, hoping.

00:21:43.308 --> 00:21:46.029
You know, pondering how you wish it was

00:21:46.029 --> 00:21:49.612
is the single biggest
waste of time in life

00:21:50.917 --> 00:21:52.391
and in business.

00:21:52.391 --> 00:21:54.263
Like, the bottom line is

00:21:54.263 --> 00:21:55.308
we all wish things.

00:21:55.308 --> 00:21:58.500
I wish I was 6'4, the single
best looking guy in the world

00:21:58.500 --> 00:21:59.954
and the quarterback of the New York Jets

00:21:59.954 --> 00:22:01.794
that has won 19 straight Super Bowls.

00:22:01.794 --> 00:22:03.028
I wish that.

00:22:03.028 --> 00:22:04.611
I mean I wish that.

00:22:05.543 --> 00:22:06.973
I mean, I just wish that.

00:22:06.973 --> 00:22:08.083
I wish that.

00:22:08.083 --> 00:22:11.105
And in the same way that that's funny

00:22:11.105 --> 00:22:13.448
is the same way to wish that
like, that deal went through

00:22:13.448 --> 00:22:15.462
or that employee took the job

00:22:15.462 --> 00:22:16.665
or that person didn't quit

00:22:16.665 --> 00:22:19.145
or that concept didn't work out.

00:22:19.145 --> 00:22:21.624
I mean, I've had multiple divisions

00:22:21.624 --> 00:22:25.603
multiple strategies here
at Wine, at Wine Library.

00:22:25.603 --> 00:22:27.492
At VaynerMedia that haven't worked

00:22:27.492 --> 00:22:28.427
in the last 12 months.

00:22:28.427 --> 00:22:29.964
Yet, we're dismantling it.

00:22:29.964 --> 00:22:32.096
And so much like anything else

00:22:32.096 --> 00:22:34.608
it's like the next, it's the next play.

00:22:34.608 --> 00:22:37.666
Using artistic stuff, like if
you're doing a Broadway play.

00:22:37.666 --> 00:22:40.770
What are you going to mess
up a line and just stop?

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:42.354
And just cry on stage?

00:22:42.354 --> 00:22:44.135
You just got to go to the next line.

00:22:44.135 --> 00:22:45.413
I mean, you just got
to keep moving forward

00:22:45.413 --> 00:22:47.386
and so how do I handle it?

00:22:47.386 --> 00:22:49.966
Easily, I'm a (beep) champ on this issue.

00:22:49.966 --> 00:22:50.922
I'm a champion.

00:22:50.922 --> 00:22:53.716
I am an absolute champion on this issue.

00:22:53.716 --> 00:22:56.890
Meaning like, I'm prepared
for the punches in the face.

00:22:56.890 --> 00:23:00.237
I expect things not to go well.

00:23:00.237 --> 00:23:02.389
It's kind of the way I
prep for a Jets game.

00:23:02.389 --> 00:23:03.980
Saturday night, nobody
likes being around me

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:06.132
because I'm just devastated, it's all bad.

00:23:06.132 --> 00:23:07.454
We're going to lose, this
is what's going to happen.

00:23:07.454 --> 00:23:08.287
We're going to lose.

00:23:08.287 --> 00:23:10.819
You know, like I'm
subconsciously like that.

00:23:10.819 --> 00:23:13.800
But unlike the Jets where
I say that outwardly

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:15.093
these guys can tell you

00:23:15.093 --> 00:23:17.764
I'm only on winning on
offense at all times.

00:23:17.764 --> 00:23:18.838
- [JoeWhitmarsh] Adam asks

00:23:18.838 --> 00:23:20.045
"I feel like I've lost my hustle.

00:23:20.045 --> 00:23:21.516
"Help, what do I do?"

00:23:21.516 --> 00:23:24.155
- Adam, anybody who's losing their hustle

00:23:24.155 --> 00:23:25.201
has a lot of variables.

00:23:25.201 --> 00:23:26.674
And there's a lot of
variables in this episode.

00:23:26.674 --> 00:23:27.674
This is the variable episode

00:23:27.674 --> 00:23:28.560
where there's a lot of contact sports.

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:29.925
There's not just direct answers today.

00:23:29.925 --> 00:23:33.049
Listen, you might not be passionate

00:23:33.049 --> 00:23:34.575
about your north star anymore.

00:23:34.575 --> 00:23:36.983
I've been going through something.

00:23:36.983 --> 00:23:39.132
Let me actually bring
some shattering news here.

00:23:39.132 --> 00:23:41.825
For the first time in my life

00:23:41.825 --> 00:23:44.488
I've been thinking about weird things

00:23:44.488 --> 00:23:48.613
like sabbaticals and
building a business school

00:23:48.613 --> 00:23:50.548
in Haiti for five years

00:23:50.548 --> 00:23:51.965
and like, I love.

00:23:55.157 --> 00:23:57.611
Listen, I'm still all about
buying the New York Jets

00:23:57.611 --> 00:23:59.538
and it's the process of that and the game

00:23:59.538 --> 00:24:02.127
but it's amazing for me
to be in tune with myself.

00:24:02.127 --> 00:24:03.660
The maturity as you get older.

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:05.466
Just like my kids are six and three

00:24:05.466 --> 00:24:06.741
and they're interesting.

00:24:06.741 --> 00:24:08.385
Like they're interesting.

00:24:08.385 --> 00:24:10.084
And like, I start projecting.

00:24:10.084 --> 00:24:12.096
What do they look like at 13 and 15?

00:24:12.096 --> 00:24:13.591
It would be really cool to like

00:24:13.591 --> 00:24:14.692
I don't believe in the school system

00:24:14.692 --> 00:24:15.623
as much as everybody.

00:24:15.623 --> 00:24:17.826
Do I just take them and
go do something rogue?

00:24:17.826 --> 00:24:20.450
Just different things, you just evolve.

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:23.507
And you might have just
lost your north star.

00:24:23.507 --> 00:24:25.105
The thing that you
thought you were going for

00:24:25.105 --> 00:24:25.938
might not be.

00:24:25.938 --> 00:24:26.771
It's why I was always happy

00:24:26.771 --> 00:24:27.812
that I didn't want a million bucks

00:24:27.812 --> 00:24:29.032
or I didn't want fame

00:24:29.032 --> 00:24:30.873
or I didn't want a car or private plane.

00:24:30.873 --> 00:24:31.853
All these little things.

00:24:31.853 --> 00:24:34.082
It was fun to be like,
"I want to buy the Jets."

00:24:34.082 --> 00:24:34.945
And what that always meant to me

00:24:34.945 --> 00:24:36.732
was I want the process of buying the Jets.

00:24:36.732 --> 00:24:38.012
Which means I get to hustle forever

00:24:38.012 --> 00:24:39.837
because that's my, that's who I am.

00:24:39.837 --> 00:24:41.937
Maybe you've lost who you are

00:24:41.937 --> 00:24:42.770
or what you want

00:24:42.770 --> 00:24:43.999
or maybe you've accomplished kind of.

00:24:43.999 --> 00:24:45.763
Or maybe you feel it's achievable.

00:24:45.763 --> 00:24:48.613
I would be crushed if I thought
what I wanted to achieve

00:24:48.613 --> 00:24:50.180
was super achievable.

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:51.352
I mean, it happened to
me in the wine business.

00:24:51.352 --> 00:24:52.461
It's happening at Vayner.

00:24:52.461 --> 00:24:54.651
Like, as Vayner is becoming a player

00:24:54.651 --> 00:24:55.694
in the agency world

00:24:55.694 --> 00:24:56.681
and we're not like ugh.

00:24:56.681 --> 00:24:57.618
Now, we're like oh.

00:24:57.618 --> 00:24:59.512
Like, and soon we're going to be like ah.

00:24:59.512 --> 00:25:00.679
Like you know?

00:25:01.786 --> 00:25:02.820
That's not as fun for me.

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:03.933
I like being the underdog.

00:25:03.933 --> 00:25:05.870
I like the climb you know?

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:06.870
I like that.

00:25:07.827 --> 00:25:09.924
So, maybe that's happened.

00:25:09.924 --> 00:25:11.983
Maybe you're just tired.

00:25:11.983 --> 00:25:14.046
I mean, I think one of the
things that the New York Jets do

00:25:14.046 --> 00:25:15.105
for me that nobody understands

00:25:15.105 --> 00:25:16.355
is those hours.

00:25:17.306 --> 00:25:19.309
Actually, you just caught
something on DailyVee right?

00:25:19.309 --> 00:25:20.142
Me talking to Jeff.

00:25:20.142 --> 00:25:21.336
That was the first time I talked business

00:25:21.336 --> 00:25:22.876
during a Jet's game in 15 years.

00:25:22.876 --> 00:25:24.145
I looked at my phone at the wrong time.

00:25:24.145 --> 00:25:24.978
It was a very big thing.

00:25:24.978 --> 00:25:26.416
And I only spent a second on it.

00:25:26.416 --> 00:25:28.807
Like, I am escaped and relaxed.

00:25:28.807 --> 00:25:30.114
And by the way, I didn't care about it.

00:25:30.114 --> 00:25:31.176
Like that's how excited.

00:25:31.176 --> 00:25:33.186
Those three, four hours of escapism.

00:25:33.186 --> 00:25:34.690
Maybe you need some vacation time.

00:25:34.690 --> 00:25:36.440
Maybe you need to look
at the people around you.

00:25:36.440 --> 00:25:37.306
Maybe you need a change.

00:25:37.306 --> 00:25:38.139
Maybe you need to break up with your

00:25:38.139 --> 00:25:38.985
boyfriend or girlfriend.

00:25:38.985 --> 00:25:41.028
There's a lot of intense, different things

00:25:41.028 --> 00:25:42.903
that could be happening.

00:25:42.903 --> 00:25:44.421
Or maybe you could take a step back

00:25:44.421 --> 00:25:45.752
and really listen to this statement.

00:25:45.752 --> 00:25:48.544
Which is, you've lost your hustle

00:25:48.544 --> 00:25:50.506
because you've really are not in tune

00:25:50.506 --> 00:25:51.969
to why you were doing it.

00:25:51.969 --> 00:25:53.621
Right? You're just not in
tune to what you're doing.

00:25:53.621 --> 00:25:56.034
That you're maturing and realizing

00:25:56.034 --> 00:25:57.901
like, a Ferrari.

00:25:57.901 --> 00:25:59.494
Or a phat watch.

00:25:59.494 --> 00:26:01.375
Or court side tickets.

00:26:01.375 --> 00:26:03.188
Or custom Nikes.

00:26:03.188 --> 00:26:04.621
I just wanted to show them.

00:26:04.621 --> 00:26:06.588
You know, is not what you're living for.

00:26:06.588 --> 00:26:07.421
And so...

00:26:08.813 --> 00:26:10.063
You know it's funny tonight I'm going to

00:26:10.063 --> 00:26:12.239
the Charity Water Gala.

00:26:12.239 --> 00:26:14.392
I never thought of myself 10 years ago

00:26:14.392 --> 00:26:16.452
as somebody who would be
so involved in non-profits.

00:26:16.452 --> 00:26:18.503
Sitting on the board of
Pencils to Promise, donating.

00:26:18.503 --> 00:26:19.697
I was you know, I was old school.

00:26:19.697 --> 00:26:20.686
I'm 40, I'm older.

00:26:20.686 --> 00:26:22.721
Like, this new narrative
of much better people

00:26:22.721 --> 00:26:24.077
and that's what I think you guys all are.

00:26:24.077 --> 00:26:25.682
I think you're a much better generation.

00:26:25.682 --> 00:26:28.504
I grew up thinking I'll get rich

00:26:28.504 --> 00:26:30.180
and when I'm old I'll be
like, giving out money

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:31.013
and doing that stuff.

00:26:31.013 --> 00:26:32.478
So you just evolve right?

00:26:32.478 --> 00:26:34.704
And so maybe you, like me

00:26:34.704 --> 00:26:36.159
like I've haven't gone completely

00:26:36.159 --> 00:26:37.269
like I don't have enough money.

00:26:37.269 --> 00:26:38.876
My kids can give away all the money

00:26:38.876 --> 00:26:41.031
but I can't, I don't have that luxury yet.

00:26:41.031 --> 00:26:43.211
But maybe you want to do non-profit.

00:26:43.211 --> 00:26:44.631
Maybe you want to build schools

00:26:44.631 --> 00:26:45.612
for Pencils of Promise

00:26:45.612 --> 00:26:46.882
and we'll take you to Laos.

00:26:46.882 --> 00:26:48.825
Maybe you've lost your purpose.

00:26:48.825 --> 00:26:51.610
So take a good step back, shut down.

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:53.957
Shut down for 24, 48, 72 hours.

00:26:53.957 --> 00:26:54.790
Go away.

00:26:54.790 --> 00:26:56.820
Go away like this.

00:26:56.820 --> 00:26:57.735
This is how I want you to shut down.

00:26:57.735 --> 00:26:58.941
Go to Airbnb.

00:26:58.941 --> 00:27:00.710
This is the 2016 way to shut down.

00:27:00.710 --> 00:27:04.787
Go to Airbnb, find a very remote place.

00:27:04.787 --> 00:27:06.575
You know, time, do I have to go?

00:27:06.575 --> 00:27:07.719
Oh I'm keeping something,
yeah I'm just wrapping up

00:27:07.719 --> 00:27:08.868
the show, sorry.

00:27:08.868 --> 00:27:12.531
Go to Airbnb, find a very remote place

00:27:12.531 --> 00:27:14.553
that you can get to at a low cost.

00:27:14.553 --> 00:27:18.145
Like a crappy cabin far
away on a four hour drive

00:27:18.145 --> 00:27:19.641
where you can just drive because you can

00:27:19.641 --> 00:27:20.779
or like, somebody will drive you there

00:27:20.779 --> 00:27:21.946
or hitchhike, I don't give a crap.

00:27:21.946 --> 00:27:25.001
Lowest cost, furthest away, seclusion.

00:27:25.001 --> 00:27:26.486
And just be with yourself

00:27:26.486 --> 00:27:28.053
and start talking to yourself for real.

00:27:28.053 --> 00:27:29.901
For like, really talk
to yourself for real.

00:27:29.901 --> 00:27:31.433
One of the great things I do with myself

00:27:31.433 --> 00:27:33.248
is I talk to myself for real.

00:27:33.248 --> 00:27:35.564
And really, nobody is a harsher critic

00:27:35.564 --> 00:27:37.661
and a bigger fan of themselves than me.

00:27:37.661 --> 00:27:41.639
And I think that friction
in both directions matters.

